I have found that in the early to mid game, the scores produced from
such unpredictable behaviour as YASDs have a tendancy to be
innacurate. Why? Well its because you've left all your junk at a base
camp some levels up, and it wasnt included in your score. Dangit.

Consider the scenario where you are killed while still packratting
around looking for a good spot to lay down camp. Its not uncommon (at
least not in my games) to get a *higher* score if i died at this
point , than i would if i was a few thousand turns on, after id set up
camp. This seems a little innacurate since my lower level early game
character sits higher in my high score list than one that actually got
further in the game.

My answer is thus: #base. It can be used in the main dungeon only (not
in branches or in gehennom), and marks one tile (or possibly a room?)
as a 'base' square and everything on that square is considered a part
of your bases' stash. If you die, objects on this square that would
give points as if you were carrying them, only with 1/2 or maybe 1/3
of their normal point score. Creating a base on a second square in the
dungeon removes the old one, so you can only have one at a time. If
you ascend, its not considered part of your score at all. The marked
square has no effect on anything else, ie doesnt confer or restrict
elbereth or monster behaviour. A possible problem i can see are score
mongerers collecting everything in the dungeon into one spot then
escaping the dungeon. However, it would probably still be less effort
to score monger other ways, since you only get 1/2 credit for your
stash gear.

